.\" mailut(8) -- Mailu Tools
.TH MAILUT 8 "2026-09-17" "Mailu Tools 1.0.0" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
mailut \- Mailu queue, abuse and inbound-mail audit tooling
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mailut
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B mailut
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.br
.B mailut help
.RI [ COMMAND
.RI [ SUBCOMMAND ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mailut
is the single administrative command of Mailu Tools. It operates on a
.B Mailu
mail server running under Docker Compose and does three things:
.IP \(bu 3
inspects and cleans the Postfix queue, and investigates abusive or compromised
accounts;
.IP \(bu 3
samples queue and delivery behaviour on a timer, so an incident can be seen
after the fact;
.IP \(bu 3
records an auditable history of what the server decided about
.I inbound
mail \(em who tried to send it, whether it was rejected, greylisted, classified
as spam, accepted or delivered \(em in a local SQLite database.
.PP
The audit subsystem is evidence collection, not a mailbox. It answers questions
such as "did this server ever see mail from this sender for this customer, and
what did it do with it?".
.PP
Audit collection is
.B opt-in
and off until a scope is added. Nothing is collected for a recipient that no
scope covers.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Read configuration from
.I PATH
instead of
.IR /etc/mailut/mailut.conf .
The
.B MAILUT_CONF
environment variable has the same effect and is overridden by this option.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit. Equivalent to
.BR "mailut version" .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help. Every command and subcommand accepts
.BR \-\-help ;
.B mailut help \fICOMMAND\fR
is equivalent.
.SH COMMAND HIERARCHY
.nf
mailut
\(em\(em status                    operational summary
\(em\(em doctor                    read-only diagnostics
\(em\(em version                   installed version
\(em\(em config check | show       inspect configuration
\(em\(em db migrate | optimize     database maintenance
\(em\(em watch [--print]           sample queue/abuse indicators
\(em\(em report                    summarise recorded samples
\(em\(em ips                       external client IPs from the front log
\(em\(em queue list | drain | hold | release
\(em\(em audit add    all | domain <d> | email <e>
\(em\(em audit remove all | domain <d> | email <e>
\(em\(em audit scopes | show | stats | purge | doctor | collect | ingest
\(em\(em upgrade                   install a newer public release
\(em\(em uninstall                 remove the application
.fi
.SH AUDIT COMMANDS
.SS Scopes
Collection scopes decide which
.I inbound recipients
are audited. A scope is one of
.BR all ,
a
.BR domain ,
or an exact
.BR email
address. A domain scope means mail addressed to local recipients at that
domain; an email scope means that exact envelope recipient. A scope is never
interpreted as the remote sender.
.TP
.B mailut audit add all
.TQ
.BI "mailut audit add domain " DOMAIN
.TQ
.BI "mailut audit add email " ADDRESS
Ensure the scope is collected. Options:
.BI \-\-retention " DAYS"
(metadata retention for records created from now on),
.BI \-\-level " LEVEL"
.RB ( metadata ", " headers " or " message ),
and
.BI \-\-message\-retention " DAYS"
(only with
.BR "\-\-level message" ).
A level that the host disables in
.BR mailut.conf (5)
is rejected with an error; the request is never silently downgraded.
.IP
If the host configuration is tightened
.I after
a scope was created, the scope keeps its recorded level but collection falls
back to
.BR metadata .
That is never silent either:
.B mailut audit scopes
shows the effective level as
.RI ` "metadata (asked headers)" '
with a warning, and
.B mailut doctor
reports it.
.TP
.B mailut audit remove all
.TQ
.BI "mailut audit remove domain " DOMAIN
.TQ
.BI "mailut audit remove email " ADDRESS
Ensure the scope is
.B not
collected from now on. This records an explicit exclusion; it
.B does not delete anything.
See
.B REMOVING A SCOPE IS NOT DELETION
below.
.TP
.B mailut audit scopes
List the effective scopes.
.BI \-\-test " ADDRESS"
reports whether one address would be collected and which rule decides it.
.B \-\-json
prints the scope records.
.SS Scope precedence
The most specific applicable rule wins:
.IP
.B email
>
.B domain
>
.B all
.PP
At equal specificity, exclusion wins. When no rule applies, the recipient is
not collected. So
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit add all
    mailut audit remove domain private.example.com
    mailut audit add email monitored@private.example.com
.fi
.PP
collects every domain except
.IR private.example.com ,
and within that domain collects exactly
.IR monitored@private.example.com .
.PP
Domains and the domain part of addresses are compared case-insensitively, as
SMTP requires. Local parts are compared case-insensitively too.
.PP
A connection rejected before
.B RCPT TO
has no recipient to attribute. Such an event is collected only when the
.B all
scope is including, because no narrower scope could be shown to apply.
.SS Collection levels
.TP
.B metadata
The default. Envelope and connection metadata, the decision, SMTP response,
Rspamd score/action and Rspamd symbols. It stores
.B no
message body, no attachments, no complete RFC822 message and no arbitrary raw
header set.
.TP
.B headers
Additionally retains the complete RFC822 header section, when the Rspamd
exporter supplies it. No body.
.TP
.B message
Additionally retains the complete original message. Globally disabled by
default; see
.B allow_messages
in
.BR mailut.conf (5).
Stored messages are gzipped under
.I /var/lib/mailut/messages/YYYY/MM/DD/
with generated names and mode 0600, and are never shown in ordinary output.
.SS Querying
.TP
.B mailut audit show
Search retained events. Filters:
.BR \-\-since ", " \-\-after ", " \-\-before ", " \-\-domain ", " \-\-recipient ", "
.BR \-\-sender ", " \-\-sender\-domain ", " \-\-action ", " \-\-stage ", "
.BR \-\-queue\-id ", " \-\-message\-id ", " \-\-ip ", " \-\-symbol ", " \-\-limit .
.B \-\-since
accepts a duration
.RI ( 30m ", " 6h ", " 7d ", " 2w )
or an absolute timestamp.
.B \-\-json
emits JSON Lines: one self-contained JSON object per event, so a large result
set streams.
.TP
.B mailut audit stats
Counts by action and stage, pre-DATA rejection count, database and payload
storage size, oldest and newest record, next expiration and the most common
Rspamd symbols. Accepts the same filters.
.SS Purging
.TP
.B mailut audit purge
Delete retained evidence. Exactly one selector is required:
.BR \-\-expired ,
.BI \-\-domain " DOMAIN" ,
.BI \-\-email " ADDRESS" ,
.BI \-\-before " TIME" ,
or
.BR \-\-all .
.B \-\-dry\-run
reports the event and payload-file counts and changes nothing.
.B \-\-all
requires typing the word
.B purge
at the prompt, or
.BR \-\-yes .
.B \-\-expired
is idempotent and is what
.B mailut\-purge.timer
runs daily.
.PP
Payload files are removed before the database rows that reference them, so a
failure part-way through leaves no row pointing at a deleted file and no file
orphaned by a deleted row. Files that cannot be removed are reported and their
events are kept for the next run.
.SS Collector
.TP
.B mailut audit collect
Run the collector in the foreground. It listens for Rspamd metadata-exporter
posts on the configured address and polls the Mailu smtp container log for the
SMTP evidence Rspamd never sees. This is what
.B mailut\-audit.service
runs; logging goes to stdout and stderr, therefore to the journal.
.IP
The bind address is checked before the socket is opened. A loopback address is
accepted, as is the gateway of a Docker
.B bridge
network that the antispam container is attached to \(em an address reachable by
containers on this host and by nothing else, provided the port is not
forwarded. Anything else, including an address that cannot be verified, is
refused unless
.B \-\-allow\-remote
is given. See
.B bind
in
.BR mailut.conf (5)
for why a merely private address, a
.BR macvlan / ipvlan
gateway, or an unrelated project's bridge does not qualify.
.IP
A client must present the shared token to submit evidence. A missing or
unusable token file is a startup failure, never a silent fallback to accepting
everything; running without one requires
.B collector.allow_unauthenticated
and is warned about at every start. Create the token with
.BR "mailut audit token generate" .
.IP
.B \-\-once
performs exactly one log poll and exits, for diagnostics. It opens no socket,
so neither the bind assessment nor the token is checked: reading the smtp log
must keep working when Docker is unreachable, the antispam container is down or
the token file is broken \(em which is when the diagnostic is most wanted.
.TP
.B mailut audit token generate [\-\-force] [\-\-if\-missing]
Write a new random token to
.B collector.token_file
with mode 0600, creating the directory if needed, and print the
.B user
and
.B password
lines to add to the Rspamd exporter rule. An existing token is never replaced
without
.BR \-\-force ,
because doing so stops Rspamd submitting until its
.B password
is updated to match.
.B \-\-if\-missing
succeeds quietly when a token already exists, which is what
.B make enable
uses.
.TP
.B mailut audit token show
Print the current token on standard output, for pasting into the exporter rule.
Warnings about the file's permissions go to standard error, so they cannot end
up inside a configuration file.
.TP
.B mailut audit ingest {rspamd|postfix} [\-\-file PATH]
Ingest exporter payloads (JSON or JSON Lines) or Postfix log lines from a file
or standard input. Useful for replaying evidence and for testing.
.TP
.B mailut audit doctor
Alias for
.BR "mailut doctor" .
.SH SMTP STAGE LIMITATION
A remote server can be refused during the connection, after
.BR HELO ,
after
.BR "MAIL FROM" ,
or after
.BR "RCPT TO" ,
all of which happen
.B before
the
.B DATA
command. At that point the sender has transmitted no headers and no body.
.PP
For such an event the following fields cannot exist and are reported as
.RI ` "unavailable (rejected before DATA)" ':
.BR Subject ,
.BR Message-ID ,
the header
.B From
and
.BR To ,
and the message body.
.B mailut
never fabricates them. What is recorded is what genuinely existed: timestamp,
stage, remote IP, HELO name, envelope sender, envelope recipient, the SMTP
response code and the Postfix reason.
.SH QUEUE COMMANDS
.TP
.B mailut queue list
Summarise the Postfix queue: totals, deferred count, top senders by backlog and
the widest recipient fan-out.
.B \-\-detail
lists individual messages;
.B \-\-json
is machine readable.
.B mailut queue
with no subcommand is the same as
.BR "mailut queue list" .
.TP
.BI "mailut queue drain \-\-sender " ADDRESS
.TQ
.BI "mailut queue drain \-\-recipient " ADDRESS
Delete every queued message whose envelope sender (or recipient) is
.I exactly
that address. Matching is exact and case-insensitive:
.I example.com
never matches
.IR user@example.com .
.TP
.B mailut queue hold
.TQ
.B mailut queue release
Move matching messages into or out of the Postfix hold queue.
.PP
All three destructive queue commands support
.B \-\-dry\-run
and
.BR \-\-yes ,
and prompt for confirmation when run on a terminal without
.BR \-\-yes .
Without a terminal and without
.BR \-\-yes ,
they refuse rather than assume consent.
.PP
Removing mail from the queue does not disable the Mailu account and does not
stop new submissions. Secure the account separately.
.SH IP INVESTIGATION
.TP
.B mailut ips
Show the external client IP addresses seen in the Mailu
.B front
log, with the accounts that authenticated from each.
.BI \-\-since " DURATION"
sets the log window,
.BI \-\-user " ADDRESS"
narrows to one account,
.B \-\-all
includes private and internal addresses, and
.BI \-\-exclude " IP[,IP...]"
drops known addresses such as your own front.
.PP
The
.B smtp
container only ever sees the front proxy, so a compromised account's real
source address is only visible here. IPv4 only: an IPv6 address cannot be told
apart from a timestamp in free log text without false positives.
.SH REPORTING, WATCH AND STATUS
.TP
.B mailut watch
Take one sample of queue size, delivery outcomes, rate-limit and spam
rejections, and authenticated sender volume; evaluate it against the
.B [watch]
thresholds and record it in the database. Run by
.BR mailut\-watch.timer .
.B \-\-print
prints the sample without recording it.
.TP
.B mailut report
Summarise recorded samples over a window
.RB ( \-\-since ,
default 7d): sample and alert counts, peak queue size, peak per-sender volume
and the samples where a threshold was crossed.
.TP
.B mailut status
Concise operational status: version, configuration file, Mailu compose
location, database path, size and schema version, scope and event counts,
latest event, latest purge, message storage size, collector state and the state
of every unit this application owns.
.TP
.B mailut doctor
Read-only diagnostics. Checks the Python version, the Docker executable and
compose directory, the configured services, SQLite readability, writability and
schema, filesystem permissions, the collector port and \(em where systemd
exists \(em the units.
.IP
Two checks cover the Rspamd wiring, which is the part most often left half
finished.
.B "rspamd exporter"
looks for a
.B metadata_exporter
under
.I <compose_dir>/overrides/rspamd/
that targets this collector's port; the example shipped under the data
directory is deliberately not treated as evidence of anything, since
.B make install
always puts it there.
.B "rspamd -> collector"
makes an HTTP request to the collector
.I from inside
the antispam container, and reports "not verified" rather than success when the
container has no HTTP client.
.IP
A further check reports scopes whose collection level the host currently
disables. It changes nothing and exits
.B 4
when a critical check fails.
.SH LIFECYCLE COMMANDS
.TP
.B mailut version
Print the installed version, for example
.RI ` "mailut 1.0.0" '
or
.RI ` "mailut 1.0.0 (a1b2c3d)" '.
This works without git and without the source checkout.
.B \-\-json
additionally reports the database schema version this build expects, which is
how
.B mailut upgrade
verifies a migration: the upgrading process still has the outgoing release
loaded and must not judge the incoming one by its own constants.
.TP
.B mailut upgrade
Install a newer release from the project's public GitHub releases. No GitHub
account, token,
.B gh
command or SSH key is required, and the original clone may have been deleted.
Options:
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report installed and latest versions and exit. Contacts GitHub, changes
nothing, and does not require root. "Already current" is a success, not an
error.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Describe what would be done without touching installed files, configuration,
the database, systemd or Mailu.
.TP
.BI \-\-version " X.Y.Z"
Install a specific published release. Only a version is accepted, never a URL.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-downgrade
Permit installing an older release. Refused by default.
.TP
.B \-\-prerelease
Consider prereleases. Ignored by default: alpha, beta, rc and draft releases
are never installed by an ordinary upgrade.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not prompt. Needed for automated deployment when installed application files
have local modifications.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Show transfer and installation detail.
.RE
.IP
The sequence is: acquire the lifecycle lock; discover the release; download the
tarball and its
.B SHA256SUMS
into a private temporary directory; verify the SHA-256 (a mismatch is fatal and
cannot be overridden); unpack and validate the tree; stop the collector and
back up the database
.I only
when the schema changes; install; migrate; reload systemd; restore the previous
unit run state; verify; release the lock. Nothing from the network is executed
before it has been verified, and the application is never installed by piping a
download into a shell.
.IP
Configuration and audit data are always preserved.
.B mailut
requires the
.B make
utility on the host to install a release; installation itself is performed by
the new release's own
.BR "make install" ,
so upgrading and installing from source cannot drift apart.
.TP
.B mailut uninstall
Remove the application using its own install manifest. Entirely local: no
network, no GitHub, no checkout and no Makefile are needed. Units are stopped
and disabled before their files are removed, and only files listed in the
manifest are touched.
.RS
.TP
(default)
Remove application files; preserve
.I /etc/mailut
and
.IR /var/lib/mailut .
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-config
Also remove
.IR /etc/mailut .
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-data
Also remove
.IR /var/lib/mailut ,
which permanently deletes retained mail evidence.
.TP
.B \-\-purge
Exactly
.B \-\-remove\-config \-\-remove\-data
and nothing else.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the units that would be stopped and disabled, the files that would be
removed, and what would be preserved or removed. Changes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip confirmation.
.RE
.IP
.B \-\-remove\-data
and
.B \-\-purge
first print a summary of what is about to be destroyed and then require the
word
.B remove
to be typed. An empty line is not consent.
.SH REMOVING A SCOPE IS NOT DELETION
.B mailut audit remove
stops future collection.
.B mailut audit purge
deletes retained evidence. They are different operations and one never implies
the other. After
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit remove domain example.com
.fi
.PP
nothing further is collected for that domain, and every record already held for
it remains readable until its own
.B expires_at
passes or an explicit purge removes it.
.SH RETENTION
Every record stores its expiry when it is created, from the retention of the
scope that was in force at that moment. Changing a scope's retention therefore
affects
.B future
records only; it does not rewrite the expiry of history. Raw-message retention
may be shorter than metadata retention, in which case the stored message is
deleted while its metadata remains.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/sbin/mailut
The command.
.TP
.I /usr/local/lib/mailut/
Application library.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/mailut/
Example configuration, the Rspamd exporter example and the install manifest.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/mailut/install-manifest.json
The authoritative list of files this release owns; used by upgrade and
uninstall.
.TP
.I /etc/mailut/mailut.conf
Configuration. Never overwritten by an install or an upgrade. See
.BR mailut.conf (5).
.TP
.I /var/lib/mailut/mailut.sqlite3
Audit database, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mailut/messages/
Stored raw messages, mode 0700.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mailut/backups/
Database backups taken before a schema migration.
.TP
.I /run/mailut/lifecycle.lock
Lock serialising upgrade, uninstall and schema migration.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/mailut-*.service, mailut-*.timer
Units owned by this application.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MAILUT_CONF
Default configuration file path, overridden by
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B MAILUT_LIB_DIR
Directory containing the
.B mailut
Python package. Only needed when running from an unusual location.
.TP
.B HTTPS_PROXY, HTTP_PROXY, NO_PROXY
Honoured by
.B mailut upgrade
through the standard library HTTP client. Proxy credentials are never stored.
.TP
.B MAILUT_ROOT
Re-bases every installed path under one directory, so the process cannot reach
the real installation at all. Because nothing privileged is then in reach, the
root requirement of
.B upgrade
and
.B uninstall
does not apply under it. A testing aid used by the test suite for staged
installs; do not set it on a real system.
.TP
.B MAILUT_UPSTREAM_API
Redirects release discovery. Honoured
.B only
when
.B MAILUT_ROOT
is also set, so it applies to a staged test installation and can never point a
real installation's upgrade at a different upstream.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Operational or runtime failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line or invalid argument value.
.TP
.B 3
A destructive action was not confirmed.
.TP
.B 4
A verification or doctor check failed.
.TP
.B 5
Another lifecycle operation holds the lock.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Mail metadata is sensitive. The database and the message store are mode 0600
and 0700 and owned by root.
.PP
The collector accepts audit evidence over HTTP, so it is protected at two
levels, neither of which is sufficient alone.
.PP
.B Who can reach it.
It binds to 127.0.0.1 by default. The Rspamd exporter runs inside a container
and cannot reach the host's loopback, so the documented setup binds instead to
the gateway of a Docker bridge network the antispam container is attached to
\(em an address reachable by containers on this host and by nothing else,
provided the port is not forwarded. That test is deliberately narrow: a merely
private address may be a LAN or VPC address reachable by every machine on that
network; a
.B macvlan
or
.B ipvlan
gateway is normally the real upstream router and is not covered by the packet
filtering Docker applies to bridge networks; and an unrelated project's bridge
is not reachable from Mailu at all. An address that cannot be verified is
refused rather than assumed safe, as is a wildcard or publicly routable bind,
unless
.B \-\-allow\-remote
is given deliberately. Request bodies are size-limited.
.PP
.B Who may submit.
Ingestion requires a shared token by default, presented as HTTP Basic
credentials (what Rspamd's metadata_exporter can send) or as a Bearer header.
The collector refuses to start without one rather than accepting anonymous
submissions, because a trail that any process able to reach the port can write
to is not evidence; opting out takes a deliberate
.BR collector.allow_unauthenticated ,
which
.B mailut doctor
warns about for as long as it is set. Create the token with
.BR "mailut audit token generate" ;
it must not be readable by other accounts, and one that is, is refused rather
than used. The unauthenticated
.I /health
endpoint exposes liveness only; its counters require the token.
.PP
Ingested values \(em senders, recipients, HELO names, subjects, Rspamd symbol
options and Postfix rejection text \(em are chosen by the remote side. They are
never interpolated into a shell command (every external program is run as an
argument vector), never used to build a filesystem path, and terminal control
characters are escaped before anything is printed. All SQL uses bound
parameters.
.PP
Complete message retention is disabled by default and must be enabled
deliberately in the configuration file. Retained bodies are never printed by
ordinary list output.
.PP
.B mailut\-audit.service
and
.B mailut\-watch.service
run as root because they must reach the Docker socket to read the Mailu queue
and container logs; a non-root user or
.B PrivateNetwork
would break that access. The units therefore apply the hardening that is
compatible with it \(em
.BR NoNewPrivileges ,
.BR PrivateTmp ,
.BR ProtectSystem=full ,
.BR ProtectHome ,
kernel and cgroup protections \(em rather than directives that would silently
prevent collection.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start collecting for one customer domain, keeping a year of metadata:
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit add domain customer.example --retention 365
.fi
.PP
Collect everything except one internal domain, but keep one monitored address
inside it:
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit add all
    mailut audit remove domain internal.example.com
    mailut audit add email monitored@internal.example.com
    mailut audit scopes
.fi
.PP
Investigate a customer who says a supplier's mail never arrived:
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit show --recipient john@example.com \e
                      --sender supplier@example.net --since 7d
.fi
.PP
Find everything an RBL rejected in the last month, as JSON Lines:
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit show --symbol RBL_SPAMHAUS --since 30d --json
.fi
.PP
Clean a compromised account's queue, checking first:
.PP
.nf
    mailut queue drain --sender compromised@example.com --dry-run
    mailut queue drain --sender compromised@example.com
    mailut ips --since 6h --user compromised@example.com
.fi
.PP
Preview and then run a retention purge:
.PP
.nf
    mailut audit purge --expired --dry-run
    mailut audit purge --expired --yes
.fi
.PP
Upgrade:
.PP
.nf
    mailut upgrade --check
    sudo mailut upgrade
.fi
